### Figure vs Axes-Level Functions

**Axes-Level Functions** (return matplotlib Axes):
```python
# Lower level, more control
fig, ax = plt.subplots()
sns.scatterplot(data=data, x='x', y='y', ax=ax)

# Can combine multiple on same axes
sns.scatterplot(data=data1, x='x', y='y', ax=ax)
sns.scatterplot(data=data2, x='x', y='y', ax=ax)

# Works directly with Matplotlib
ax.set_title('My Plot')
```

**Figure-Level Functions** (return FacetGrid):
```python
# Higher level, automatic layout
g = sns.relplot(data=data, x='x', y='y', hue='group')

# Creates subplots automatically for faceting
g = sns.relplot(data=data, x='x', y='y', hue='group', col='category')

# Returns object with figure and axes
g.fig  # access figure
g.axes  # access axes array
```

**When to Use Each:**
```
Axes-level:
- Single plot
- Combined with Matplotlib
- Full control needed
- Within existing figure

Figure-level:
- Faceted plots (multiple subplots)
- Quick exploration
- FacetGrid functionality needed
- Publication-quality by default
```

