## 🎯 Figure-Level vs Axes-Level

**Quick Comparison:**

| Aspect | Axes-Level | Figure-Level |
|--------|-----------|-------------|
| Function | `scatterplot`, `barplot` | `relplot`, `catplot`, `displot` |
| Returns | matplotlib Axes | FacetGrid object |
| Faceting | Manual with subplots | Built-in with `col`, `row` |
| Ease | Familiar to Matplotlib users | Quick exploration |
| Integration | Works with Matplotlib | FacetGrid specific |
| Best For | Single plot, fine control | Multiple subplots |

**Axes-Level Example:**
```python
fig, axes = plt.subplots(1, 2, figsize=(12, 5))

# Plot 1
sns.scatterplot(data=data1, x='x', y='y', ax=axes[0])
axes[0].set_title('Data 1')

# Plot 2
sns.scatterplot(data=data2, x='x', y='y', ax=axes[1])
axes[1].set_title('Data 2')

plt.tight_layout()
```

**Figure-Level Example:**
```python
# Automatic subplots
g = sns.relplot(data=data, x='x', y='y', col='group', hue='category')

# Access elements
g.fig.suptitle('Overall Title')
g.axes[0, 0].set_title('Custom Title')
```

