g.set_axis_labels('Sales', 'Product')

# Solution 4: Box plot (better for many categories)
fig, ax = plt.subplots(figsize=(12, 8))
top_products = sales_data.groupby('product')['sales'].median().nlargest(15).index
sns.boxplot(data=sales_data[sales_data['product'].isin(top_products)],
           y='product', x='sales', ax=ax)
ax.set_title('Solution 4: Box Plot (Top 15 Products)')

# Solution 5: Count plot with sorting
fig, ax = plt.subplots(figsize=(10, 8))
product_order = sales_data['product'].value_counts().head(15).index
sns.countplot(data=sales_data[sales_data['product'].isin(product_order)],
             y='product', order=product_order, ax=ax)
ax.set_title('Solution 5: Frequency of Top 15 Products')
ax.set_xlabel('Count')
```

**Decision Guide:**

| Problem | Solution |
|---------|----------|
| Too many categories | Filter to top N |
| Hard to read labels | Use horizontal orientation |
| Want comparisons | Use faceting (col/row) |
| Want to see distribution | Use box/violin plot |
| Space constraints | Use smaller figure with rotated labels |

**Best Practices:**
- 🔑 Filter to top N categories (10-15 typically readable)
- 🔑 Use horizontal orientation for text readability
